From: overpressure <overpressure@pico4>
Subject: [PATCH] media: venus: a decoder client's 16 capture buffers stand

Steam Link's client asks for 16 capture buffers and has images for 16. Venus raised the count to the
firmware's minimum, 18 for a Main10 stream at 1280x5120, so that the client went on to queue buffers
without an image: the first frame arrived, QBUF of buffer 16 failed with EINVAL, and the stream stuck.
The firmware decodes the same stream with 16 or 12 of the 18 queued (40 frames, no errors). The
minimum still goes to the firmware's check; a request below 16 is still raised.
---
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/vcodec/venus/vdec.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/vcodec/venus/vdec.c
@@ -893,6 +893,8 @@
 	return ret;
 }
 
+#define VDEC_CAPTURE_BUFS_ENOUGH	16U
+
 static int vdec_num_buffers(struct venus_inst *inst, unsigned int *in_num,
 			    unsigned int *out_num)
 {
@@ -992,8 +994,13 @@
 						    inst->width,
 						    inst->height);
 		inst->output_buf_size = sizes[0];
-		*num_buffers = max(*num_buffers, out_num);
-		inst->num_output_bufs = *num_buffers;
+		inst->num_output_bufs = max(*num_buffers, out_num);
+		/*
+		 * The firmware asks for more than a client may have images for: Steam Link's has 16 and
+		 * asks for 16, where the firmware's minimum for its stream is 18. It decodes with fewer
+		 * queued, so a client's 16 stand.
+		 */
+		*num_buffers = max(*num_buffers, min(out_num, VDEC_CAPTURE_BUFS_ENOUGH));
 
 		mutex_lock(&inst->lock);
 		if (inst->codec_state == VENUS_DEC_STATE_CAPTURE_SETUP)
